#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130096} :activity/id #uuid "3c707632-71cb-5aef-87e4-616ace4c62a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049267 :topic/id #uuid "e85b4a6b-2f1c-5e42-8b55-1cd17da31c61" :topic/math-academy-id 2025} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130408 :tutorial/content "The **mass density function** $\\lambda (x,y)$ of a two-dimensional shape describes how its mass is distributed across the shape. \n\nMore precisely, $\\lambda(x,y)$ tells us the *mass per unit area* at any point $(x,y)$ on the shape. The units of $\\lambda$ are usually $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2.$\n\nSuppose we want to determine the total mass of the semi-circular metal plate $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/da/da2a02c6d172b6fef36b317bfb89d0c4ccf3be8533446bce9908cfd8f4ad6840.png)\n\n The small rectangle shown has mass $\\delta M$ equal to its average density (which we can assume is approximately $\\lambda (x,y)$) times the area of the piece.\n\n$$\n\\delta M = \\lambda (x, y)\\, \\delta x \\delta y =  \\lambda (x, y) \\, \\delta A\n$$\n\n \n\nBy summing all contributions to the mass made by each small rectangle and taking the limit as $\\delta A \\to 0$ in the usual way, we find that the mass of the entire plate is given by\n\n$$\nM = \\iint \\limits_{D} \\lambda(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nNote that since mass is never negative, we must have $\\lambda \\geq 0$ for $\\lambda$ to be a valid mass density function." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc73413b-4b1e-5be9-8f3f-64a297c09923" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4903 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0f057ff0-a365-502d-858f-384c3235b99d" :step/math-academy-id 30620 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130409 :tutorial/content "We can also use density functions to describe the distribution of electric charge across a metallic plate. \n\nThe distribution of electric charge is given by its **charge density function,** denoted $\\rho(x,y).$ The units of $\\rho$ are $\\textrm{C}/\\textrm{m}^2,$ where $\\textrm C$ is the standard unit of electric charge, known as the **coulomb**.\n\nIn general, the total electric charge $Q$ of a two-dimensional region $D$ with the charge density function $\\rho(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_D \\rho(x,y) \\: \\mathrm d A.\n$$\n\nSince electric charge can be positive *and* negative, $\\rho$ can be any real number." :tutorial/id #uuid "96166249-d79b-586e-b871-4e5d4f6deb84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4909 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Electric Charge Over a Metal Plate"} :step/id #uuid "715aeebb-3507-5529-b0c5-6a47c374827f" :step/math-academy-id 30634 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129208 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129027 :question/id #uuid "27249c57-7f17-5e3e-9a21-0518320f5076" :question/math-academy-id "e-8192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360576}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"} :step/id #uuid "52d6d2d0-732b-5818-9395-cc60fc0d64cf" :step/math-academy-id 30646 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129209 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129028 :question/id #uuid "d565cc1a-f78f-5712-8a03-df4d4e8f3e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-8193"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/i